From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artyom Shalkhakov <artyom.shalkhakov@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0500
Subject: [PATCH] Honour [c] and [d] in equality comparisons

The [c] and [d] options are honoured by every comparison except the one
people write most often. -[GSComparisonPredicate evaluateWithObject:]
works out the string compare options from the predicate's options and
then uses them only for the ordering and matching operators: == and !=
are answered with -isEqual:, which can express neither option. "Ada"
==[c] "ada" is therefore false, where OS X says true.

The option is parsed and kept - the predicate even prints back as name
==[c] ada - so the mismatch is silent, and a filter that reads as case-
insensitive simply misses rows.

Compare two strings with the options when either is set, and leave
everything else going through -isEqual:, so nothing that is not a pair
of strings changes behaviour.

A reproduction program accompanies this patch; it exits 1 on an
unpatched library and 0 with the change.
---
diff --git a/Source/NSPredicate.m b/Source/NSPredicate.m
index dabf91d1c..572b628e0 100644
--- a/Source/NSPredicate.m
+++ b/Source/NSPredicate.m
@@ -1525,9 +1531,30 @@ GSICUStringMatchesRegex(NSString *string, NSString *regex, NSStringCompareOption
   switch (_type)
     {
       case NSEqualToPredicateOperatorType:
-	return [leftResult isEqual: rightResult];
       case NSNotEqualToPredicateOperatorType:
-	return ![leftResult isEqual: rightResult];
+	{
+	  BOOL	equal;
+
+	  /* The [c] and [d] options apply to equality as well as to
+	   * ordering and matching: "Ada" ==[c] "ada" is true.  -isEqual:
+	   * can express neither option, so two strings compared with one
+	   * are compared as strings.
+	   */
+	  if ((_options & (NSCaseInsensitivePredicateOption
+	    | NSDiacriticInsensitivePredicateOption))
+	    && [leftResult isKindOfClass: [NSString class]]
+	    && [rightResult isKindOfClass: [NSString class]])
+	    {
+	      equal = ([leftResult compare: rightResult
+				   options: compareOptions] == NSOrderedSame);
+	    }
+	  else
+	    {
+	      equal = [leftResult isEqual: rightResult];
+	    }
+
+	  return (NSEqualToPredicateOperatorType == _type) ? equal : !equal;
+	}
       case NSMatchesPredicateOperatorType:
 #if	GS_USE_ICU == 1
 	return GSICUStringMatchesRegex(leftResult, rightResult, compareOptions);
